summary:
Xu and Zhu construct the p-adic companion of the Kloosterman sheaves of Heinloth–Ngô–Yun. For a split reductive group Ǧ, the Frenkel–Gross connection Be_Ǧ = d + (N + λ^h xE)dx/x on G_m receives a unique Frobenius structure φ ∈ Ǧ(A^†) whose traces are those of Kl_Ǧ (Theorem 4.4.4); the resulting Bessel F-isocrystal Be^†_Ǧ generalises Dwork's Bessel F-isocrystal Be_n^†. The proof runs Heinloth–Ngô–Yun's geometric Langlands construction with arithmetic D-modules, for which the paper first develops complements to the six-functor formalism (§2: specialisation maps, equivariance, weights, nearby cycles, universal local acyclicity, local monodromy, Braden's hyperbolic localisation) and proves the geometric Satake equivalence for arithmetic D-modules, with and without Frobenius structures (§3). It then compares the de Rham and rigid Kloosterman objects by specialisation on minuscule and quasi-minuscule Schubert varieties (§4.2) and identifies the de Rham one with the Frenkel–Gross connection by strengthening Zhu's theorem (§4.3). Applications: the geometric and arithmetic monodromy groups of Be^†_Ǧ and Kl_Ǧ, with G_2 and SO_{2n+1} appearing for SL_{2n+1} at p = 2 (Theorem 4.5.2), local monodromy at ∞ (Corollary 4.5.7), Heinloth–Ngô–Yun's functoriality conjecture (Theorem 5.1.4), hypergeometric descriptions and exponential-sum identities for classical groups (§5.3), ordinarity of Frobenius slopes for classical groups and G_2 (Theorem 5.4.2), and a 2-adic proof of Carlitz's identity through Dwork's congruences and Tsuzuki's minimal-slope theorem (Appendix A).
The atlas has no arithmetic D-modules, no Kloosterman sheaves for reductive groups and no Frenkel–Gross connection. Routes:
- a Part II of PadicDifferentialEquationsAndRigidCohomology for arithmetic D-modules, their six operations and weights, nearby cycles, ULA, hyperbolic localisation, and geometric Satake with p-adic coefficients (the geometry of Beilinson–Drinfeld Grassmannians and Mirković–Vilonen cycles is planned in GlobalShtukasAndFunctionFieldLanglands GS.1);
- a new roadmap for Kloosterman sheaves and Bessel F-isocrystals of reductive groups, carrying the paper's main theorems;
- Deligne's Kloosterman sheaf, the Kloosterman connection and Katz's monodromy groups join the pending KloostermanMomentsAndPotentialAutomorphy (PAPER-FRESAN-SABBAH-YU-22), and Tsuzuki's theorem joins the pending minimal-slope Part II (PAPER-TSUZUKI-23);
- sources for RD stages where the paper uses planned p-adic foundations with extra precision.
Thirty source issues, each checked by hand or by exhaustive computation. The main ones:
- two exponential-sum identities of the introduction are wrong as printed;
- the convolution formula for SO_{2n+1} misses a factor −1/√q (a half Tate twist);
- the toric formula for Kloosterman sums of SO_{2n} has the wrong constant for n ≥ 4, because the proof misses a stratum;
- Mirković–Vilonen's dimension of T_λ ∩ Gr_μ is misquoted;
- the normality needed for Braden's theorem fails for Schubert varieties when p divides |π_1(G_der)|;
- Corollary 2.4.3 on top-degree cohomology and Lemma 2.5.3 on equivariance are false as stated;
- the proof of Proposition 2.4.4 reduces to a point by a base change that is not available;
- the sign conventions for the Dwork and Kummer F-isocrystals disagree between §1, §2.1.5 and §4.1.13;
- misprints in the monodromy theorem, Lemma 4.5.5, §5.1, §§2–3 and the appendix.
